# the boom graphics facade: the Renderer, passes, and their resources
#
# A game draws through boom handles alone and never imports the backend
# libraries. Texture, Mesh, Material, RenderTarget, and the Renderer wrap
# mach-vk, mach-glfw, mach-image, and mach-gltf state that stays hidden
# here. texture_load decodes PNG, QOI and TGA images and mesh_load extracts
# glTF (.glb) geometry, neither exposing a backend type.
#
# THE RENDERER IS THE CORE ABSTRACTION (boom.graphics.renderer). A game
# creates one against its window, then each frame calls
# renderer_begin_frame, runs one or more passes, and renderer_end_frame,
# which submits and presents. A pass targets the window or an offscreen
# RenderTarget, carries a perspective camera (3D) or a pixel-space
# orthographic projection (2D), and submits mesh or sprite draws. 2D and
# 3D go through the same machinery.
#
# RESOURCES ARE CREATED AGAINST A DEVICE. Vulkan has no implicit current
# context the way GL did, so texture_load, mesh_load, model_load, and
# render_target take the Device that renderer_device returns. That is the
# largest shape difference a game sees.
#
# SHADERS ARE PIPELINES, NOT PROGRAMS. A Vulkan pipeline bakes both stages
# and the whole fixed-function state into one immutable object, so there
# is no per-pass or per-material shader to set. A pipeline is chosen by
# what a draw is: a mesh, a skinned mesh, or a sprite. The renderer builds
# them on demand and keeps them (boom.graphics.cache), keyed by that
# choice and by the mesh's vertex layout, because Vulkan bakes the vertex
# input into the pipeline too. A game supplies its own stages through
# boom.graphics.shader instead, which is the supported way to do lighting:
# boom ships one lit program as a default and does not intend to grow a
# lighting system, since how a surface responds to light is the game's
# decision. See examples/shader. The shaders are Mach, compiled to SPIR-V by
# the build-shaders step and embedded by boom.graphics.spirv; they live in
# src/shaders/.
#
# AN IMMEDIATE-MODE DRAW LIST GOES THROUGH pass_draw_triangles. A sprite is one
# rectangle placed by a model matrix, which is the wrong shape for a UI, a
# particle system or a debug overlay: those produce thousands of triangles that
# are already in the right place, each with its own colour, rebuilt every frame.
# Those go in one draw, through a per-frame vertex arena, in the layout
# vertex_format_ui describes. blit emits exactly that layout.
#
# EVERY DRAW TAKES A UNIFORM SLOT. A Vulkan draw reads a buffer range that
# must already hold its values when the command buffer executes, so each
# draw writes its own slot of a per-frame ring. A frame therefore has a
# bounded number of draws, and renderer_dropped reports any it refused
# rather than letting them silently not appear.
#
# A mesh is described by a VertexFormat (boom.graphics.vertex): a declared
# list of attributes rather than a fixed vertex struct, and a pipeline
# derives its vertex input state from that same format so the two cannot
# disagree. mesh_load conforms a glTF primitive to one of two canonical
# layouts, standard or skinned, filling in attributes the asset omits,
# because the shaders declare fixed inputs and a shader input with no
# attribute behind it is invalid rather than merely unused.
#
# Skeletal animation builds on that: model_load returns a Model (a skinned
# Mesh, a Skeleton, and named Animation clips) with mach-gltf fully
# hidden, and model_player(name) gives an AnimationPlayer. The pose
# runtime lives in boom.graphics.skeleton and boom.graphics.animation, and
# pass_draw_skinned uploads the pose's joint matrices to a storage buffer
# the skinned vertex stage reads.
#
# Failures return Result[T, Error]; Error carries its message inline
# (boom.graphics.error), so no failure needs a heap allocation or a
# global. Geometry math comes from boom.math. Consumers import this as
# `use boom.graphics;`.

